					<p class="body1" align="left"><span class="body1 style73"> <h2>News archive</h2><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-28 | Thursday</font></strong><br><br>Peace, yall. Day 362 of this crazy year. First off, all respect to the late Godfather of Soul,<strong> James Brown</strong>. The man&#39;s been through some high and low lights over the years, but his legacy as a performer will forever be etched into the spirit of what hip-hop was and will always be. Rest in power.<br /><br />Did yall get your tickets for the MassLine New Year&#39;s Eve and New Year&#39;s Eve Eve parties yet? Larry &quot;Gatsby&quot; Mizell, Jr. gave us a shout in this week&#39;s <a href="http://www.thestranger.com/seattle/Content?oid=125342" title="The Stranger">My Philosophy column in The Stranger</a>, also highlighting other New Year jumpoffs going on around the town. <a href="http://seattleweekly.com/music/0652/shortlist.php">Seattle Weekly listed the show on their Short List</a>, big upping <strong>Gabriel Teodros</strong>&#39; <em>Lovework</em> album as an early &quot;favorite for 2007.&quot; Tickets are goin&#39; fast, but are still available at <a href="http://www.ticketswest.com" title="TicketsWest">Ticketswest</a>. Hurry up and buy!<br /><br />And always coming through with the goods, the town&#39;s own <strong>DJ Hyphen</strong> just dropped the new mixtape <em>Beats Rhymes and Life Vol. 6: From the Soul</em>, which he&#39;s generously made available for <a href="http://www.sendspace.com/file/duv52a" title="DJ Hyphen">download right here</a>. But you can also cop a hard copy at Platinum Records and other fine 206 record stores real soon.<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-25 | Monday</font></strong><br><br>i drove around for an hour last night looking for some place open to get food. wasn&#39;t in the mood to cook and i kinda wanted a cheeseburger. it was like &quot;damn! why is it that every place i drive by is closed?&quot;<br /><br />then it was like &quot;oh right, that holiday that a bunch of people celebrate is today.&quot;<br /><br />sall good tho.  just don&#39;t expect me to update the blog on <strong>Eid</strong>.<br /><br />lucky for me, Purple Dot was able to serve me up some chicken chow mein.<br /><br /><br />tomorrow is our first full 9 person rehearsal with <strong>Big World Breaks</strong>.  the music sounds very very very filthy.  grab your tickets for the <strong>New Year&#39;s Eve</strong> show now before they sell out!<br /><br /><img src="http://www.dustyhawk.com/images/eidfitr.jpg" alt="eid" title="eid" width="460" height="300" /><br /><br />i&#39;m a couple months late i know, but to everybody reading, have a good holiday.<br /><br />Saba<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-22 | Friday</font></strong><br><br><strong>Looking for something to do Friday night?</strong>! look no further... we got you.<br /><br />Seattle soul-sensation <strong>Choklate</strong> will be doing a charity event at the <strong>Chop Suey </strong>along with <strong>Zachary Bruce</strong> and DJ <strong>DV One</strong>.  Doors are at 9PM... $10 or $8 with a can of food.<br /><br />There have been some updates on DV One&#39;s case with the SPD... you can find <a href="http://blog.myspace.com/index.cfm?fuseaction=blog.view&amp;friendID=1697410&amp;blogID=199838096">more information here</a><br /><br />Also on Friday night... you might have seen the posters around town asking <strong>&quot;Who Is Suntonio Bandanaz?!&quot;</strong>  The question will be answered 8PM at <strong>Xiamaca&#39;s</strong> (on 2nd Ave. between Bell &amp; Blanchard).  This is the <strong>Suntonio Bandanaz Release Party</strong> featuring performance by <strong>Mind Movers</strong> and Beacon Hill&#39;s favorite beastmaster dj <strong>WD4D</strong>.  The Suntonio Bandanaz album, if you haven&#39;t heard, has got some heated guest appearances including <strong>1st Platoon</strong>, <strong>SKIM</strong>, <strong>Macklemore</strong>, and your mom&#39;s favorite group <strong>Abyssinian Creole</strong>.<br /><br />We will see you @ <strong>Neumos</strong> for <strong>New Years Eve</strong>... hit up <a href="http://ticketswest.rdln.com/Artist.aspx?evt=51855">ticketswest.com</a> for those tickets now!<br /><br />-- gt<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-21 | Thursday</font></strong><br><br>word on the virtual streets is that there is a new bill proposing changes to the State of Washington Liquor Laws.  included in the proposal is the <strong>elimination of all-ages shows</strong> at venues with alcohol.  you can read more and discuss this <strong><u><a href="../smf/index.php?topic=657.new;boardseen#new">in the forum</a></u></strong>.  if this is true, i can tell you that my personal opinion on this involves the word <strong>&quot;booooo&quot;</strong> with extra o&#39;s.<br /> <br /><strong>maintaining a vibrant music scene in the community</strong> that youth can interact with and participate in <strong>is crucial</strong> to the development of our city.  young folks still listening to the radio talkin bout &quot;<em>my favorite rapper is whooptie whoo from a city that isn&#39;t seattle</em>,&quot; or &quot;<em>how come nobody is really coming up in the game reppin the town?</em>&quot;<br /><br />the answer to all these questions involves addressing the accessibility of our music scene.  this means being able to go to shows, to perform, to learn about the business; to have a place where young folks can just gather creatively without being held back, either by the institutions of the city or by people in the scene themselves.<br /><br />feel free to contact the following people and let them know what you think about this:<br /><br />-<u><strong><a href="http://www.seattle.gov/mayor/citizen_response.htm">Mayor Greg Nickels</a></strong></u><br />-your <u><strong><a href="http://apps.leg.wa.gov/memberemail/Default.aspx">State Representatives</a></strong></u><br />-Pam Madsen at the Liquor Board. [wslcb@liq.wa.gov]<br />-Seattle City Attorney Tom Carr. [thomas.carr@seattle.gov]<br /><br />apparently, some people seem willing to keep the city drunk but aren&#39;t too interested in encouraging creative expression among youth.  <strong>that leads to too many ideas</strong>!<br /><br />saba<p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><strong><font color="#333300" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2">2006-12-20 | Wednesday</font></strong><br><br>Back online after six days, three without power. Reading and writing by candlelight wore out it&#39;s novelty after Saturday and whole pockets of the Southend are still in the cold and dark. All that good food we stocked up on in the fridge, wasted. The studio&#39;s basement got flooded and along with it all the beats for our next album. Sike, nothing was lost in the flood but  some furniture. The beats are safe. Still f**ked up tho.<br /><br />What hits home even harder is the tragedy of folks lost in the storm&#39;s aftermath: one homie&#39;s family friend drowned in a basement, one of the teacher homie&#39;s student <a href="http://seattlepi.nwsource.com/local/296438_power19.html">lost his family to carbon monoxide poisoning</a>. We&#39;re dying to stay warm, literally, and that&#39;s not wassup. No one is to blame for bad weather. But times like these pull the veil off the divide real fast. Hope fades to frustration when you realize that your<br />neighborhood is looking like the set of <em>28 Days Later</em> while less than a mile away in Seward Park, folks are getting warm, getting on with their lives. Big up to the workers braving the cold, laboring to get the wires back up and trees off the road. But to the policymakers and such, especially those seeking <a href="http://seattlepi.nwsource.com/national/1152AP_Iraq_Spending.html?source=mypi">$99,700,000,000 more to fund the war</a> while people in THIS country aren&#39;t getting their basic shit covered, f**k to you!! (in accented english).<br /><br />And to top it all off, another storm rolls through the Northwest tonight. Might be another six days til you hear from a brotha. Til then, I think I&#39;ll play an appropriate soundtrack for the times: <strong>Sleep of Oldominion&#39;<em>s</em></strong><em> Riot By Candlelight LP</em> (2001) and <strong>Silent Lambs Project&#39;s</strong> <em>Souliquor LP</em> (1999).<div><br /></div><div>peace,</div><div>Geo<br /><br /></div><p></p><br><hr align="center" noshade size="1" width="400"><br><center><div class="pagenumbers"><A HREF="archive.php?from=245">[&lt;&lt;]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=0">[1]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=5">[2]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=10">[3]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=15">[4]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=20">[5]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=25">[6]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=30">[7]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=35">[8]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=40">[9]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=45">[10]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=50">[11]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=55">[12]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=60">[13]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=65">[14]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=70">[15]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=75">[16]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=80">[17]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=85">[18]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=90">[19]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=95">[20]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=100">[21]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=105">[22]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=110">[23]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=115">[24]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=120">[25]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=125">[26]</A> <A HREF="archive.php?from=130">[27]</A>